    "bottomLine": "The top choice for exterior and above-grade block where you want a natural look and strong water repellency.",
    "description": "Ghostshield Siloxa-Tek 8500 is a penetrating silane-siloxane water repellent, and for most above-grade block it is the product to beat. The blend makes sense for block: siloxane handles the large surface pores of an open-textured unit while silane reaches deeper, and together they line the pores with a water-shedding treatment instead of a film. The result is a wall that looks unchanged but sheds rain, which reduces the cycle of soaking and freezing that cracks face shells and the wicking that leaves efflorescence. Ghostshield markets it for concrete, block, brick and pavers, inside and out. The main limit is the one every repellent shares: it is not a barrier against water pushed through the wall from saturated soil, so on a seeping basement wall it is a supplement to drainage and a waterproof coating, not a substitute. It also tends to prevent paint adhesion, so decide on color before you seal. Owners commonly report strong beading on exterior block. Expect roughly $100 to $200 for a typical container at the time of writing."
  },
  {
    "id": "drylok-extreme-masonry-waterproofer",
    "name": "DRYLOK Extreme Masonry Waterproofer",
    "category": "Masonry Waterproofing Coating",
    "badge": "Best for Basement Block",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Maker rates it to withstand 15 psi of hydrostatic pressure",
      "Fills block pores and pinholes when brushed in",
      "Works as both moisture barrier and finish coat",
      "Tintable and widely available"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Changes the look of the wall",
      "Needs bare masonry free of paint and efflorescence",
      "Brush application on block is slow and tiring"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best sealer for interior basement block that seeps or stays damp after rain.",
    "description": "On the inside of a basement block wall, a repellent is the wrong tool because the water arrives from behind. DRYLOK Extreme is built for exactly that situation. It is a thick masonry waterproofer that the manufacturer rates to withstand 15 psi of hydrostatic pressure, which is well above what most residential walls with working gutters see. The key is application: the label calls for scrubbing the first coat into the block with a stiff nylon brush until every pinhole is filled, then adding a second coat. On block, that scrubbing matters more than on poured concrete, because the open texture is full of voids a roller will skip. DRYLOK Extreme dries to a paint-like finish that can be left as is or tinted, so it handles moisture and appearance at once. Its limits are the usual ones for coatings: it cannot stop water at the floor joint, and it fails early over efflorescence or paint. Expect roughly $40 to $60 a gallon at the time of writing."
  },
  {
    "id": "foundation-armor-sx5000",
    "name": "Foundation Armor SX5000",
    "category": "Silane-Siloxane Penetrating Sealer",
    "badge": "Best Value Repellent",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Silane-siloxane blend for deep and surface protection",
      "No visible film or change in texture",
      "Helps protect against water, salt and freeze-thaw damage",
      "Offered in water-based and solvent-based versions"
