    ],
    "bottomLine": "A strong-value penetrating repellent for exterior block, garage walls and retaining wall faces.",
    "description": "Foundation Armor SX5000 is a silane-siloxane penetrating sealer that competes directly with Ghostshield and often costs a little less. It works the same way: the blend soaks into block, brick and concrete and leaves a water-repellent treatment inside the pores rather than a coating on top, so the surface looks unchanged and continues to breathe. Foundation Armor sells versions with different carriers; the water-based version is easier to live with indoors and in tight spaces, while solvent-based sealers can penetrate dense surfaces more readily but need careful ventilation and ignition control. It is well suited to exterior block walls, garage walls and the exposed faces of retaining walls, where rain and splash are the main threats. Like any repellent, it is not designed to stop water pushed through a basement wall from wet soil, and it may prevent later painting. Owner feedback commonly mentions easy application with a pump sprayer and good beading on porous surfaces. At the time of writing, five-gallon pails often run roughly $150 to $250."
  },
  {
    "id": "radonseal-deep-penetrating",
    "name": "RadonSeal Deep-Penetrating Concrete Sealer",
    "category": "Reactive Silicate Penetrant",
    "badge": "Best Silicate Option",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Reacts inside the concrete rather than coating it",
      "No film to peel, blister or wear off",
      "Treats walls and slabs with one product",
      "Detailed instructions for testing absorption"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Performs best on poured concrete with free lime; results on block vary",
      "Needs unsealed, unpainted surfaces",
      "No visible change makes progress hard to judge"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A good pick for mixed basements with block walls and a poured slab, where you want a film-free treatment.",
    "description": "RadonSeal Deep-Penetrating Concrete Sealer takes a different route from the silane-siloxane products on this list. It is a water-based silicate-type sealer that reacts with lime and calcium compounds inside concrete to form a gel that fills capillaries and pores, reducing the movement of water and vapor without leaving anything on the surface. The maker says it penetrates deeply into concrete. That reaction depends on having enough reactive material in the pores, which is why it tends to perform best on dense poured concrete and somewhat less predictably on lean, hollow block. Where it shines is a basement with both block walls and a poured slab, or a wall where you want no film at all. It must go on bare, unsealed concrete; RadonSeal publishes instructions for checking absorption before you start. Despite the name, it is not a radon mitigation system on its own, and the EPA treats sealing as a supplement to active mitigation. Expect roughly $80 to $130 for a gallon at the time of writing."
  },
