  {
    "id": "ames-block-wall",
    "name": "Ames Block & Wall Liquid Rubber",
    "category": "Elastomeric Block Coating",
    "badge": "Best Flexible Coating",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Flexible film tolerates hairline cracks and joint movement",
      "Water-based with water cleanup",
      "Formulated for masonry and concrete block",
      "Can be painted over after curing"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Thicker film means lower coverage per gallon",
      "Interior use on pressurized walls can blister",
      "Needs multiple coats to reach full thickness"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A good option for block walls with hairline mortar cracks where you want a flexible coating rather than a repellent.",
    "description": "Ames Block and Wall is a water-based elastomeric coating aimed at concrete block. Its strength is flexibility: block walls develop hairline cracks along mortar joints as they settle and move with temperature, and a rigid coating can crack along with them. An elastomeric film built to the recommended thickness stretches over that movement and keeps the wall sealed. That makes it useful on exterior block, garage walls and retaining wall faces where you want a coating instead of an invisible repellent, and on interior walls that are damp from condensation or light wicking. It is less suited to interior basement walls with water pushing through from the soil side, where a flexible film can lift; a cement-based masonry waterproofer is the safer choice there. Ames recommends multiple coats to build thickness, and coverage per gallon is lower than with a thin paint. Prep is the same as for any coating. It can be painted once cured if you want a specific color. Expect roughly $70 to $110 a gallon at the time of writing."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Cinder Block Sealers",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you seal a cinder block wall?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Clean the wall, remove efflorescence and old coatings, repoint damaged mortar and let it dry. For exterior or above-grade walls, spray a penetrating silane-siloxane repellent from the bottom up until it saturates, often in two wet-on-wet coats. For interior basement walls that seep, brush two coats of a masonry waterproofer into the bare block instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much water does concrete block soak up?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, readily. Concrete block is made with a lean, open-textured mix and has hollow cores, so it absorbs and transmits water much more than poured concrete. Water that passes through the face shell can collect in the cores and travel down the wall. That is why block walls benefit from sealing and why drainage around them matters."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I seal a painted block wall?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not with a penetrating sealer; it needs bare masonry to soak into and cannot pass through paint. Most masonry waterproofers also require bare surfaces to perform as rated. Strip the paint by grinding, chemical stripping or abrasive cleaning first, or, if the paint is sound and the wall dry, simply repaint with a masonry paint system."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often does block need resealing?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Penetrating repellents commonly last five to ten years in typical exposure, depending on product, sun and weather. Masonry waterproofers on interior walls also typically last five to ten years before touch-ups. The bead test tells you when to reapply: if water darkens the block and soaks in rather than beading, the treatment has worn through."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does block need sealing before it gets painted?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not with a penetrating repellent, which can prevent paint from bonding. Before painting, use a block filler primer, which seals and smooths the surface. If the wall is a basement wall with dampness, a masonry waterproofer can serve as both the moisture barrier and the finish, or be topcoated with latex paint after it cures."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is DRYLOK a good cinder block sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For interior basement block that seeps or stays damp, yes. DRYLOK is a masonry waterproofer rated by its maker for hydrostatic pressure and designed to be brushed into block pores. For exterior above-grade block where you want a natural look and full breathability, a penetrating silane-siloxane repellent is usually the better choice."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you seal block in cold weather?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most sealers specify a minimum air and surface temperature, often around 40 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, during application and for a period afterward. Cold slows curing and can cause water-based products to fail. Check the label, avoid sealing when frost is forecast overnight, and choose a stretch of mild, dry weather for exterior walls."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
