          <a href="#prep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Prepping Block</a>
          <a href="#apply" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. How to Apply</a>
          <a href="#coverage" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Coverage and Quantity</a>
          <a href="#painting" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Sealing and Painting</a>
          <a href="#lifespan" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. When to Reapply</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. What It Costs</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">13. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Concrete block, still called cinder block by almost everyone, soaks up water far faster than poured concrete. Its open texture and hollow cores mean a sealer has a lot more surface to treat and a lot more ways for water to get around it. There are two good ways to seal block: soak it with a penetrating repellent that leaves it looking unchanged, or brush on a thick waterproof coating. Which one is right depends on where the wall is and what water it faces.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 7 Best Cinder Block Sealers, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are ranked on what the manufacturers publish (active ingredients and solids, coverage on block, whether the product is rated for water pressure, recoat and cure times), how well each chemistry suits porous block specifically, and patterns in owner feedback. We did not test these products ourselves; specifications and claims are attributed to the makers.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="block-basics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Block Walls Need a Different Approach
          </h2>

          <p>
            A standard 8-by-8-by-16-inch concrete masonry unit is mostly air. Two or three hollow cores run through it, the face shells are only about an inch thick, and the concrete itself is a lean, open-textured mix. Water that gets through a face shell lands in a core, runs down inside the wall, and shows up somewhere else, often at the bottom course where it meets the floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mortar joints add another path. A block wall has a joint every 8 inches vertically and every 16 inches horizontally, and mortar is often more porous than the block. Hairline cracks between mortar and block are common, especially in older walls and where the wall has settled. A sealer has to treat both the block face and the joints to do any good.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compared with poured concrete, block also tends to show efflorescence more readily and to wick water further up the wall. That is why a sealer that works well on a dense poured wall can underperform on block: there is less material to react with and more open pore space to fill. Products made or labeled specifically for masonry and block usually account for this with higher solids or recommended flood coats.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not all block is the same either. Smooth-faced standard block, split-face decorative block and lightweight block made with expanded aggregates all absorb differently. Rougher and lighter block drinks more product. Check the label for block-specific coverage rates, which are usually much lower than the headline figure for smooth concrete.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="two-families" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Repellent or Coating: The First Decision
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Penetrating water repellents</strong> soak into the block and line the pores with a water-shedding compound. They leave no visible film, so the wall looks the same, breathes, and has nothing to peel. They are excellent at stopping rain and splash from soaking in, which is why they are the standard choice for above-grade exterior block, retaining walls and garage walls. Their limitation is pressure: they repel water but are not designed to resist water pushed through the wall from wet soil behind it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Waterproof coatings</strong> such as DRYLOK and elastomeric products form a film on and in the surface. Cement-and-latex masonry waterproofers fill the pores and are rated by their makers to resist a stated hydrostatic pressure. They change the look of the wall, since they go on like thick paint, and they need a clean, bare surface to bond. They are the standard choice for basement block that seeps.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple rule covers most cases. Above grade, where water hits the face of the wall, choose a penetrating repellent. Below grade on the inside of a basement, where water arrives from the soil behind the wall, choose a waterproof coating. Our guide to the <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">best basement wall sealers</a> goes deeper on the below-grade side.
          </p>

