        <div id="chemistry" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Silane, Siloxane and Blends Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most penetrating repellents for block are silanes, siloxanes or a blend. <strong>Silanes</strong> are very small molecules that penetrate deep into dense concrete and react with moisture in the pores to form a water-repellent lining. <strong>Siloxanes</strong> are larger molecules that do not travel as deep but work well in more porous materials, like block and brick, and tend to cure faster at the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because block is porous, a blend of silane and siloxane is a common and sensible choice: the siloxane handles the open surface pores, and the silane reaches deeper. Many products on this list are blends. Manufacturers often list the active solids percentage; higher is not automatically better, but very low solids products may need more coats to perform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Silicate sealers, such as RadonSeal, work differently. They react with the lime in concrete to form a gel or crystalline structure in the pores, densifying rather than repelling. They tend to perform best on poured concrete with plenty of free lime and somewhat less predictably on block. Water-based and solvent-based versions of repellents both exist; water-based are lower odor and easier for interior use, while solvent-based often penetrate dense surfaces more readily.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the chemistry, these products only work if they can get into the block. Paint, old sealers, efflorescence and dirt all block penetration. If water still beads on part of the wall before you seal it, that area has an existing treatment and will not absorb new product until it is stripped or worn away.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK CHECK</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Splash water on the wall before you buy.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If it darkens and soaks in, the block will accept a penetrating sealer. If it beads, something is already on the surface and must be removed first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="location" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sealing Block by Location
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Basement walls.</strong> Interior basement block faces water from the soil side. A masonry waterproofer brushed into bare block is the usual pick for seepage, with a penetrating repellent as an option for walls that are merely damp and that you want to leave unpainted. Fix outdoor drainage first; our guide on <a href="/how-to-waterproof-a-basement/">how to waterproof a basement</a> covers the order.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Exterior above-grade walls and garages.</strong> Rain is the main enemy here. A silane-siloxane repellent stops the block from soaking up water, which reduces freeze-thaw damage, efflorescence and staining. It leaves the block looking natural. If you want color, use a masonry paint system instead; see our guide to the <a href="/best-paint-for-cinder-block/">best paint for cinder block</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Retaining walls and planters.</strong> These are tricky because water arrives from both sides: rain on the face and saturated soil behind. Ideally, the back of a retaining wall is waterproofed with a membrane and drained with gravel and a pipe when it is built. On an existing wall, a penetrating repellent on the exposed face helps, but efflorescence will continue if the soil behind it stays wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Block pools, ponds and tanks.</strong> Water containment needs a dedicated system, usually a cementitious or elastomeric waterproofing designed for immersion, sometimes with a liner. Standard repellents are not designed for constant submersion. Follow the coating maker's immersion guidance or use a product sold for that purpose.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Prepping Block: Efflorescence, Mortar and Patching
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the mortar. Rake out loose or crumbling joints and repoint them with a fresh mortar or a masonry patch, then let it cure for the time on the bag. New mortar is very alkaline and some coatings call for a waiting period, often several weeks, before sealing. Fill open voids and holes in the block faces with a patching compound.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remove efflorescence. Dry-brush the white deposits with a stiff brush, then clean with a masonry cleaner or a mild acid product made for efflorescence, following its dilution and safety instructions. Rinse well. If efflorescence keeps coming back quickly, water is still moving through the wall, and you should look at drainage before sealing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Strip any paint or previous coating where you plan to use a penetrating sealer; it cannot pass through a film. For a masonry waterproofer, the label usually requires bare masonry too. On exterior walls, a pressure washer at moderate pressure helps clean block, but keep the tip back from the surface so you do not erode mortar joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the wall dry. Most penetrating sealers want a surface-dry wall, often 24 to 48 hours after washing or rain, while some water-based products tolerate a damp surface; read the label. Air and surface temperatures should usually be above 40 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit and below the maximum on the label, with no rain expected for at least a day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Apply a Penetrating Sealer to Block
          </h2>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers are best applied with a low-pressure pump sprayer, the kind used for garden chemicals, with a fan or cone tip. Spray from the bottom of the wall upward so the run-down saturates the area below without leaving streaks. Many makers call for a flood coat: enough product to create a 6-to-8-inch run-down below the spray line.
          </p>

          <p>
            On porous block, a second coat applied wet-on-wet, before the first has dried, is often recommended. Follow the label: some products want the second coat within minutes, others after a set time. Back-roll with a thick-nap roller or brush on rough block to push sealer into mortar joints and the textured surface. Protect windows, plants and adjacent surfaces from overspray.
          </p>

          <p>
            For masonry waterproofers, the method is different. Stir thoroughly, then brush the first coat hard into the block with a stiff nylon brush, working it into every pinhole and mortar joint. Apply the second coat after the label dry time. Rolling a first coat onto block almost always leaves pinholes, and those pinholes are where water gets through.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventilate well indoors, especially with solvent-based sealers, and wear eye protection, gloves and a respirator rated for the product. Keep ignition sources away from solvent vapors. Clean water-based products from tools with water while still wet; solvent-based products need the cleanup solvent named on the label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coverage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Coverage and How Much to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Measure the wall area, length times height, and subtract large openings. Then use the block-specific coverage from the label, not the headline figure. Penetrating repellents on standard block often land in the range of roughly 75 to 200 square feet per gallon per coat, depending on porosity and whether the maker calls for a flood coat. Split-face and lightweight block can absorb far more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Masonry waterproofers also cover less on block than on smooth concrete. DRYLOK lists roughly 75 to 100 square feet per gallon for the first coat on smooth masonry, with rough block taking more. For two coats on 500 square feet of block wall, plan on about 10 to 12 gallons in total.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Block Sealing by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">75-200</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, penetrating</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">75-100</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Sq ft per gallon, waterproofer coat 1</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Coats on most block</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">24-48 hrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical dry time before sealing</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Test a small area first. Apply sealer to a few square feet, note how much it drinks, and let it cure. Then splash water on it. If the water beads and the block stays light, the coverage rate you used works. If it darkens, apply more or add a second coat. This also confirms that the product does not darken or stain your particular block.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="painting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Sealing Before or Instead of Painting
          </h2>

          <p>
            Painting and penetrating sealers often do not mix. Many silane-siloxane repellents leave the block surface water-repellent enough that paint will not bond to it. If there is any chance you will want to paint the wall later, check the product data sheet for paint compatibility before applying. Some repellents state they can be painted over after a cure period; many say nothing or warn against it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Masonry waterproofers sit at the other end. They are designed to be the finish or to accept a latex topcoat after cure, and they come tinted in many colors. On an interior basement wall that needs both moisture control and a decent look, a tinted waterproofer can do both jobs in one product.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your goal is primarily appearance, with color, smooth texture and easier cleaning, you want a paint system rather than a sealer: a block filler primer to close the pores, followed by an acrylic masonry topcoat. Our guide to the <a href="/best-paint-for-cinder-block/">best paint for cinder block</a> covers fillers, topcoats and when a waterproofer should replace both.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never put a penetrating repellent on top of paint expecting it to help. It cannot reach the block through the paint film, and most of it will sit on the surface, leaving a slick or blotchy finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Long Block Sealers Last and When to Reapply
          </h2>

          <p>
            Penetrating repellents gradually lose effectiveness as UV light, weather and abrasion break down the treatment near the surface. Makers often quote multi-year lifespans, and figures of five to ten years are common on labels for silane-siloxane products in typical exposure, with some claiming longer. Exposed, sunny, wet walls wear faster than sheltered ones.
          </p>

          <p>
            The bead test is the easiest way to know when to reapply. Splash or spray water on the wall. If it beads and runs off, the treatment is still working. If it darkens the block and soaks in, the repellent has worn through and the wall is ready for another coat. Reapplication is usually simpler than the first time because the wall needs only cleaning, not stripping.
          </p>

          <p>
            Masonry waterproofers on interior basement walls typically last five to ten years before touch-ups, longer if the wall faces little water. Watch for blistering, flaking and returning efflorescence, all of which suggest water is pushing behind the coating. Touch up small areas after cleaning and abrading; widespread failure points back to drainage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep notes of what you used and when. Sealers from different families do not always work well on top of each other, and knowing that a wall had a silane-siloxane treatment four years ago saves you from buying a coating that will not bond.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. What Sealing a Block Wall Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, quality penetrating repellents for block typically cost roughly $30 to $70 per gallon, more for concentrates and premium silane-heavy formulas. Masonry waterproofers run roughly $35 to $60 a gallon. A typical 500-square-foot exterior garage or retaining wall might take $150 to $400 in sealer for a DIY job, plus a pump sprayer, brushes and cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional sealing of exterior block typically runs about $1 to $3 per square foot for penetrating repellents and more for waterproof coatings that need extensive prep. Interior basement coating jobs commonly price at $3 to $7 per square foot including cleaning and patching.
          </p>

