        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling a coating onto block</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A roller bridges over the open pores of block and leaves pinholes that water passes straight through. The first coat of any masonry waterproofer needs to be scrubbed in with a stiff nylon brush. Rollers are acceptable for back-rolling penetrating sealers or for a second coat on smoother block.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over efflorescence</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                White mineral deposits block penetrating sealers from entering the pores and push coatings off the wall as salts keep forming. Dry-brush, clean with an efflorescence remover, rinse and dry the wall before sealing. If the deposits return within weeks, find and fix the water source before sealing again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a repellent against pressure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Silane-siloxane repellents keep rain from soaking in, but they are not designed to stop water pushed through a basement wall from saturated soil. For interior basement block that seeps, use a masonry waterproofer with a labeled pressure rating, and fix gutters and grading outside first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the mortar joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Joints are often more porous than the block and are where many leaks start. Repoint loose or cracked mortar before sealing, and work sealer into the joints with a brush or back-roller. A sealer sprayed only on the block faces leaves the weakest part of the wall untreated.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Under-applying on porous block</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Block absorbs far more product than poured concrete, and labels often state lower coverage for it. Spreading a gallon over the smooth-concrete coverage figure leaves too little in the pores to work. Test a small area, use the block coverage rate, and apply a second coat where the label calls for one.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

