        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you seal a cinder block wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean the wall, remove efflorescence and old coatings, repoint damaged mortar and let it dry. For exterior or above-grade walls, spray a penetrating silane-siloxane repellent from the bottom up until it saturates, often in two wet-on-wet coats. For interior basement walls that seep, brush two coats of a masonry waterproofer into the bare block instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much water does concrete block soak up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, readily. Concrete block is made with a lean, open-textured mix and has hollow cores, so it absorbs and transmits water much more than poured concrete. Water that passes through the face shell can collect in the cores and travel down the wall. That is why block walls benefit from sealing and why drainage around them matters.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal a painted block wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not with a penetrating sealer; it needs bare masonry to soak into and cannot pass through paint. Most masonry waterproofers also require bare surfaces to perform as rated. Strip the paint by grinding, chemical stripping or abrasive cleaning first, or, if the paint is sound and the wall dry, simply repaint with a masonry paint system.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does block need resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Penetrating repellents commonly last five to ten years in typical exposure, depending on product, sun and weather. Masonry waterproofers on interior walls also typically last five to ten years before touch-ups. The bead test tells you when to reapply: if water darkens the block and soaks in rather than beading, the treatment has worn through.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does block need sealing before it gets painted?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not with a penetrating repellent, which can prevent paint from bonding. Before painting, use a block filler primer, which seals and smooths the surface. If the wall is a basement wall with dampness, a masonry waterproofer can serve as both the moisture barrier and the finish, or be topcoated with latex paint after it cures.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is DRYLOK a good cinder block sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For interior basement block that seeps or stays damp, yes. DRYLOK is a masonry waterproofer rated by its maker for hydrostatic pressure and designed to be brushed into block pores. For exterior above-grade block where you want a natural look and full breathability, a penetrating silane-siloxane repellent is usually the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal block in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most sealers specify a minimum air and surface temperature, often around 40 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit, during application and for a period afterward. Cold slows curing and can cause water-based products to fail. Check the label, avoid sealing when frost is forecast overnight, and choose a stretch of mild, dry weather for exterior walls.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

